// Enum variants as ordinary VALUES, and the binder forms that must keep
// working beside them.
//
// The v1 emitter gives each variant a file-scope `static constexpr`, and
// two variant names cannot have one: a name another emitted C++ symbol
// owns, and a name more than one enum declares (no single index is
// right). Those are suppressed and diagnosed at the use site, which is
// what lets a program merely DECLARE the clash and still build — this
// file is that program.
//
// The declaration/use split is the whole design, and it is invisible to
// the unit tests, which assert on emitted text and never run a compiler.
// It is also invisible to every other fixture: this is the only one in
// the corpus that declares a duplicate variant, so without it the
// suppression logic and the binder gate ship untested by anything that
// compiles or executes.
//
// Registered in the TB-IR equivalence set, so v1 and the default backend
// must agree on all of it.

// `SLVERR` and `OKAY` are each declared by BOTH enums, so neither has a
// single correct index as a value: a bare use is rejected on BOTH
// backends (v1 in harc#662, TB-IR in harc#666 — before which TB-IR
// folded it first-wins and silently returned the wrong number). This
// file therefore never uses one as a value. Constraint position is the
// exception: it resolves through the solver's own first-wins table, so
// the `keep resp == OKAY` below is legal and both backends agree on it —
// which is exactly why the value-position rejection stops at values.
enum RdResp { OKAY, SLVERR }
enum WrResp { SLVERR, OKAY }

// Unambiguous variants are unaffected and usable as values. An
// ambiguous one is NOT usable as a value at all — it has no definition —
// so every value-position use below names one of these.
enum Color { RED, GREEN, BLUE }
enum Sev { LOW, MID, HIGH }

// The other three suppression rules, each present as a DECLARATION only.
// Every one of these is legal and must build: that is the whole point of
// diagnosing at the use site. Each also breaks the C++ compile the moment
// its rule regresses, which is what makes them worth carrying here —
// `A`/`Pkt`/`main` would then get file-scope definitions and collide,
// and nothing in the unit tests would notice because none of them
// compiles what it asserts on.
//
//   * `Dup` repeats `A` inside ONE enum. Suppression does not apply (one
//     enum, so an index IS well defined); the emission loop dedups it.
//     Regression → `redefinition of 'A'`.
enum Dup { A, B, A }
//   * `Named` collides with the `Pkt` transaction below, which emits a
//     `struct Pkt`. Regression → "must use 'struct' tag to refer to
//     type 'Pkt'" at the `let p : Pkt` further down.
enum Named { Pkt, ELSEWHERE }
//   * `Stage` collides with `main`, which the backend emits into every
//     file. Regression → "main cannot be declared as a variable in the
//     global scope". (`setup`/`teardown` would be the natural siblings
//     but are reserved words, hence `alpha`/`omega`.)
enum Stage { alpha, main, omega }

transaction Pkt
    addr : uint<8>
end transaction Pkt

transaction Req
    sev  : Sev
    resp : RdResp
    // Uses an ambiguous name in constraint position only. Must solve to
    // RdResp's index for `OKAY` (0) under the documented first-wins rule.
    keep resp == OKAY
end transaction Req

// A covergroup bin naming an UNAMBIGUOUS variant (`GREEN`, declared only
// by `Color`) still lowers on both backends — the harc#666 rejection of a
// bin like `{OKAY}` keys on multiple-declaration, not on a variant
// appearing in a bin. The ambiguous case cannot live in a passing fixture
// (it is rejected and does not compile), so it is pinned by the tbir unit
// tests; this is its accepting complement, exercised end-to-end.
covergroup ColorCov @(posedge dut.clk)
    cp : cover dut.count_out
        bins
            g = {GREEN}
        end bins
end covergroup ColorCov

testbench EnumScopeTb
    dut : Top
    cov : ColorCov
end testbench EnumScopeTb

impl EnumVariantScopeTest for EnumScopeTb
    run
        // An enum-typed local with an initializer — the shape that used
        // to emit `VColor* c = BLUE`, a Verilator handle for a module
        // that does not exist.
        let picked : Color = BLUE
        assert picked == BLUE
            else fail("enum-typed local lost its initializer")
        assert picked != RED
            else fail("bare variant compared wrong in expression position")

        // A bare variant assigned into an enum-typed record field.
        let r : Req
        r.sev = HIGH
        assert r.sev == 2
            else fail("record field assignment from a bare variant is wrong")

        // Binder forms named after a SUPPRESSED variant. Each hides the
        // file-scope name in C++, so each is a legal program and none may
        // draw the suppressed-variant diagnostic. Every one of these was
        // a spurious rejection at some point while this landed.
        //
        // Note these names may not ALSO appear in value position anywhere
        // in this file: the binder set is collected file-globally and is
        // deliberately permissive, so a binder here suppresses the
        // diagnostic everywhere. That costs a missed diagnostic, never a
        // wrong value — the cheap direction, by design.
        let OKAY : uint<8> = 7
        assert OKAY == 7
            else fail("a local named like a suppressed variant was mangled")

        for SLVERR in 0 .. 2
            assert SLVERR >= 0
                else fail("a for-variable named like a suppressed variant broke")
        end for

        // Constraint position over the ambiguous names still resolves.
        for i in 0 .. 4
            let t : Req
            randomize(t)
            assert t.resp == 0
                else fail("iter ${i}: `keep resp == OKAY` did not hold")
        end for

        // `Pkt` the TYPE still resolves, even though `Pkt` is also a
        // variant of `Named`. This is the line that fails to compile if
        // the type-collision rule regresses.
        let p : Pkt
        p.addr = 9
        assert p.addr == 9
            else fail("a record whose name is also a variant broke")

        // The third binder form: an `on` handler argument named after a
        // suppressed variant. `let` and `for` are covered above; the `on`
        // argument had two separate lowerings that each went missing.
        let ev : event<uint<8>>
        on ev(SLVERR)
            assert SLVERR == 3
                else fail("an on-handler arg named like a suppressed variant broke")
        end on
        emit ev(3)
        wait 1 cycle

        log(info, "PASS: enum variants, suppression, and binder shadowing")
    end run
end impl EnumVariantScopeTest
